---
import ToolPageShell from '../../../components/ToolPageShell.astro';
import CatAgeCalc from '../../../components/calculators/CatAgeCalc.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How old is a 1-year-old cat in human years?',
    a: 'About 15 human years. Cats mature rapidly: a six-month-old kitten is roughly comparable to a 10-year-old child, and a two-year-old cat to a person in their mid-20s.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a “cat years” formula?',
    a: 'Veterinary education charts use 15 human years for the first year, 9 for the second, and about 4 for each year after that. It is an approximation for communication, not a biological law.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the feline life stages?',
    a: 'The AAFP/AAHA Feline Life Stage Guidelines define kitten (birth to 1 year), young adult (1–6), mature adult (7–10), senior (11–14 in this tool), and geriatric (15+). Exact labels vary slightly between sources.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should my cat start senior health screening?',
    a: 'Many veterinarians recommend exams at least twice a year from around age 10, with bloodwork, blood pressure, and weight monitoring. Cats hide illness well, so regular checks catch kidney disease, hyperthyroidism, and dental disease early.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do indoor cats live longer than outdoor cats?',
    a: 'On average, yes. Indoor cats avoid traffic, fights, and many infectious diseases, and often live well into their mid-to-late teens. The calculator does not adjust for lifestyle; it only maps age to life stage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I tell my cat’s age if it is a rescue?',
    a: 'A vet can estimate age from teeth, eyes, coat, and joint condition. Estimates are more reliable for kittens and young adults than for older cats.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  {
    title: 'AAFP/AAHA Feline Life Stage Guidelines',
    url: 'https://www.aaha.org/resources/2021-aaha-aafp-feline-life-stage-guidelines/',
    publisher: 'AAHA / AAFP',
  },
  {
    title: 'AAFP Senior Care Guidelines',
    url: 'https://catvets.com/guidelines/practice-guidelines/senior-care-guidelines',
    publisher: 'American Association of Feline Practitioners',
  },
  {
    title: 'Cornell Feline Health Center: Caring for a senior cat',
    url: 'https://www.vet.cornell.edu/departments-centers-and-institutes/cornell-feline-health-center',
    publisher: 'Cornell University',
  },
];
---

<ToolPageShell
  slug="cat-age-calculator"
  title="Cat Age Calculator: Human Years & Stage"
  description="Convert your cat’s age to human years and see its AAFP/AAHA-style life stage, from kitten to geriatric. Free and private."
  answer="A cat age calculator converts your cat’s age into approximate human years and a veterinary life stage. A cat reaches about 15 human years at one, 24 at two, and then gains roughly four human years for every calendar year, following the life-stage framework used by AAFP and AAHA."
  takeaways={[
    'Year one equals about 15 human years; year two adds about 9 more.',
    'After two years, each cat year is roughly four human years.',
    'Life stage, not the human number, should guide vet visits and diet.',
    'Cats hide illness, so senior screening matters from about 10 years.',
    'Treat conversions as communication tools, not medical measurements.',
  ]}
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
>
  <CatAgeCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How to use the cat age calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your cat’s age in years. Use decimals for kittens (0.5 for six months).</li>
    <li>Read the approximate human-year equivalent.</li>
    <li>Check the life stage label and the care notes that go with it.</li>
    <li>If your cat is a rescue of unknown age, ask your vet for an age estimate first.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How cat years convert to human years</h2>
  <p>
    The conversion used here follows the most common veterinary education chart. The first year of a
    cat’s life is worth about 15 human years because kittens reach adult size and sexual maturity
    within the first year. The second year adds about nine. From the third year on, aging slows to
    roughly four human years per calendar year. Using that rule:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Cat age</th><th>Human equivalent</th><th>Life stage (tool)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>6 months</td><td>~7.5 years</td><td>Kitten</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 year</td><td>15</td><td>Prime / adult</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 years</td><td>24</td><td>Prime / adult</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5 years</td><td>36</td><td>Prime / adult</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 years</td><td>48</td><td>Mature</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 years</td><td>64</td><td>Senior</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16 years</td><td>80</td><td>Geriatric</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Feline life stages</h2>
  <p>
    The AAFP and AAHA Feline Life Stage Guidelines group cats into kitten, young adult, mature adult,
    senior, and end-of-life stages. This tool labels them in plain language as kitten, junior, prime
    adult, mature, senior, and geriatric, with boundaries at about 6 months, 1 year, 7 years, 11
    years, and 15 years. The purpose of the stages is to set expectations about nutrition, preventive
    care, and what changes to watch for. Boundaries are not hard lines: some cats are still athletic
    at 14, while others develop disease at 9.
  </p>

  <h2>What care looks like at each stage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kitten:</strong> vaccination series, deworming, spay or neuter planning, kitten food, and socialization.</li>
    <li><strong>Junior and prime adult:</strong> annual exams, dental checks, weight management, and enrichment. Neutered adults are prone to weight gain; use the <a href="/tools/cat-food-calculator/">cat food calculator</a> to set a calorie target.</li>
    <li><strong>Mature:</strong> start watching for subtle changes such as drinking more, sleeping differently, or changes in litter box habits.</li>
    <li><strong>Senior:</strong> exams twice a year, bloodwork, thyroid and kidney screening, blood pressure, and a pain check for arthritis.</li>
    <li><strong>Geriatric:</strong> comfort and quality of life, soft bedding, accessible litter boxes, and appetite support.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A 10-year-old cat: 24 + (10 − 2) × 4 = <strong>56 human years</strong>. That places the cat in
    the mature stage, about one year before the tool’s senior boundary. It is a good time to book a
    baseline senior workup, since early kidney disease or hyperthyroidism is easier to manage when
    found early.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits of any age conversion</h2>
  <p>
    Cats vary less in size than dogs, but genetics, diet, indoor or outdoor lifestyle, and dental and
    kidney health all affect how long and how well a cat lives. A well-kept indoor cat of 18 is not
    rare. Because the conversion is just a communication shortcut, it is best used to set
    expectations for care rather than to predict lifespan. If you are comparing species, try the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/dog-age-calculator/">dog age calculator</a> and read about the{' '}
    <a href="/guides/dog-years-myth/">dog years myth</a> to see why a single multiplier doesn’t work
    for either animal.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs your cat is entering its senior years</h2>
  <p>
    Age is only a guide; behavior tells you more. Watch for weight loss despite a good appetite
    (a classic sign of hyperthyroidism), increased drinking and urination (kidney disease or
    diabetes), bad breath and drooling (dental disease), stiffness when jumping, hiding, or
    changes in litter box habits. Each of these is easier and cheaper to manage when caught
    early, which is why many vets recommend a baseline blood panel and blood pressure
    check around age seven to ten, even in a cat that looks perfectly well. Keep a note of
    your cat’s weight at each visit; a drop of even half a pound matters in a small animal.
  </p>
</ToolPageShell>
